WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Speaker 1: It is being reported that Russian&nbsp;
President Vladimir Putin is readying nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.140
weapons. What does this mean? Can we trust it?&nbsp;
But maybe most importantly, what is it actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:16.620
like to theorize about what would happen if such&nbsp;
weapons were to be unleashed? So let's start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:23.940
the beginning. A number of not very good sources&nbsp;
and then some. Okay sources are reporting things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:31.320
like what Newsweek is reporting. Russia Prime's&nbsp;
nuclear bomb, 12 times more powerful than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.120
dropped on Hiroshima. Now let's break down&nbsp;
the headline, More Powerful than that dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:44.100
on Hiroshima. This is a very common 21st century&nbsp;
headline. The it's very common that now the nukes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.960
that countries have access to are more powerful&nbsp;
than the one that was dropped on Hiroshima. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.880
that doesn't tell us too, too much. But let's look&nbsp;
into the details. Video of an intercontinental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:01:00.060
ballistic missile on a silo launcher has been&nbsp;
released by Russia's Defense Ministry and widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:07.080
reported by the country's media in an apparent&nbsp;
warning to the West. A Russian tabloid newspaper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.960
reported that the yars missile complex, which&nbsp;
was loaded in the Kaluga region, had a capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:19.620
12 times greater than the American bomb that&nbsp;
destroyed Hiroshima in 1945. The mass circulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:24.660
papers report outlined some of the missile&nbsp;
specifications, including a launch weight of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.140
46,000 tons, which some are disputing as plausible&nbsp;
as implausible an operational range of up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.420
12,000 kilometers that can strike the U.S. or&nbsp;
anywhere in Europe and a payload of up to 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:42.060
kilo tons. Now, is Putin doing this and what&nbsp;
might cause him to do something like this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:48.180
We can't say with complete and total certainty.&nbsp;
So I think it's maybe more interesting and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:54.420
relevant to talk generally about what this would&nbsp;
be like, what would it look like geopolitically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.420 --> 00:02:01.980
and in terms of the science of it. If Russia&nbsp;
launched a nuke of roughly this size against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:08.820
the West. Often we just say, well, it would be&nbsp;
a general nuclear winter nuclear holocaust. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:15.000
I find that it's better to be a little more&nbsp;
specific the exact outcomes of what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.640
happen if Russia were to launch one of these&nbsp;
nukes. Let's let's put it at one for the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:28.500
being as more likely that it would be one rather&nbsp;
than a bunch just for the sake of discussion. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:35.460
consequences would really depend on the specific&nbsp;
target and and the number of weapons used. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:43.860
the potential consequences would likely include&nbsp;
the destruction of whatever city is hit and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:49.380
infrastructure of that city. So you're talking&nbsp;
buildings, you're talking roads, bridges and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:55.140
power grids already catastrophic. But those are&nbsp;
the primary impacts that we have to talk about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:01.500
the sort of like follow on effects. You then would&nbsp;
have contamination of large areas with radiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:10.320
that has significant consequences in terms of long&nbsp;
term health effects for the survivors, potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:16.080
making the areas uninhabitable. And of course, the&nbsp;
impact that that would have on the ability to grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:22.560
food. Remember, the bridges are destroyed, the&nbsp;
roads are destroyed. So if you can't grow food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.520
where do you bring food from? If the bridges and&nbsp;
the and the roads are also destroyed? So you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:36.540
see how these are compounding effects, economic&nbsp;
disruption on a global scale. Trade and financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:43.680
markets would likely be devastated. A significant&nbsp;
geopolitical escalation would likely likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.640
happen. It's sort of an understatement. I think&nbsp;
the question is to what extent and how quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:54.840
does the West respond in kind? Does this lead&nbsp;
to a full scale war involving nuclear weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.280
which these are these are just such dystopian&nbsp;
scenarios that it can be hard to think about them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.600
sort of in practical terms. And then, of&nbsp;
course, the general increase in likelihood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.800
that other countries would use their own&nbsp;
nuclear weapons and and a global nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.680
conflict that could develop. Really important to&nbsp;
understand the catastrophic nature here. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:20.580
think about just one item. Like to go back to food&nbsp;
supply. Nuclear bombing would have a catastrophic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:28.140
effect on the food supply in the affected areas.&nbsp;
The explosion itself would destroy farmland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:37.140
crops, livestock, food stores that exist. So you&nbsp;
immediately have food shortages. The contamination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:44.400
of the environment with radiation could make it&nbsp;
difficult or even impossible to grow crops, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:51.240
raise animals in those affected areas, furthering&nbsp;
the food shortages into long term. Then you've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.020
to bring food in from somewhere else. But the&nbsp;
infrastructure was destroyed, as I mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:03.540
the roads, the bridges, the ports. So transporting&nbsp;
food in becomes difficult. Exacerbating those very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:12.720
shortages. And so just to take one area food, the&nbsp;
use of a nuke could devastate the food supply and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.420
leave lead to widespread starvation. So that's&nbsp;
just food that we're talking about. How long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:25.140
would areas be affected by a nuke? The effects of&nbsp;
radiation on the environment can last for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:31.740
and it can take a significant amount of time&nbsp;
before it would be safe to live in an affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:37.320
area, to to grow food in an effective area,&nbsp;
affected area, to raise animals. It can take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:43.140
very long time. And in some cases, effects can&nbsp;
be what for the human lifespan we would call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:49.380
permanent or semi-permanent. So we don't know&nbsp;
what's going on with Putin right now. Health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:55.200
questions, questions about his mental state,&nbsp;
questions about what is the game plan here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:01.620
with what's going on in Ukraine. And we have&nbsp;
increasing but isolated and not exquisitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:08.460
well-sourced reports that nukes are being readied.&nbsp;
We have to hope, of course, it doesn't go in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:15.000
direction. And to the extent that diplomacy can&nbsp;
be used to prevent that, we should welcome it.

